  "id": "DrugBank_DB13873 - MONDO_grouped_21187_11237_11470",
  "verbalization": "Fenofibric Acid is associated with Hyperlipidemia through a series of indirect pathways involving lipid-lowering medications. The drug is linked to Obsolete Familial Combined Hyperlipidemia, which in turn is associated with Ezetimibe and Niacin, both of which are known to lower lipids. Similarly, Fenofibric Acid is connected to Familial Hyperlipidemia, which is also linked to Probucol, another lipid-lowering agent. These pathways suggest that Fenofibric Acid may exert its effects through mechanisms that target lipid metabolism, potentially by enhancing the activity of enzymes involved in lipid processing or by modulating the expression of genes related to lipid homeostasis. The evidence supports a role for Fenofibric Acid in the treatment of hyperlipidemia through its interaction with lipid-lowering drugs, which in turn contribute to the reduction of lipid levels. The integration of these pathways highlights the potential for Fenofibric Acid to be repurposed for the management of hyperlipidemia, leveraging its established pharmacological profile.",
